## Why This Is Asked

Probes automated containment and rollback for cross-region, multi-tenant Azure SaaS. Emphasizes real-time orchestration across Key Vault, AAD, RBAC, canaries, and traffic routing.

## Key Concepts

- Defender for Cloud UEBA integrations
- Event Grid and Logic Apps for automation
- Per-tenant Key Vault key rotation and token revocation
- Tenant isolation via RBAC and traffic redirection
- Canary controls and sandbox forensics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo handler for UEBA alert
async function handleUEBAAlert(event) {
  const id = event.tenantId;
  await rotateTenantKeys(id);
  await revokeTenantTokens(id);
  await setTenantRBAC(id, 'read-only');
  await pauseCanariesForTenant(id);
  await redirectTraffic(id, 'lastKnownGood');
  await cloneTenantSandbox(id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this workflow end-to-end?
- How would you audit changes made during containment?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to implement zero-trust service-to-service authentication in a multi-tenant Azure environment.

## Key Concepts
- Zero-trust service-to-service authentication using workload identities and OAuth/OIDC tokens
- Kubernetes mTLS via service mesh or sidecar proxies
- Per-tenant Azure Key Vault instances with automated key rotation
- Infrastructure-as-Code policy gates to enforce security baselines (no secrets in configuration, template signing, compliance validation)
- Comprehensive audit trails and User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) for detecting credential misuse and anomalous access patterns
- Incident response planning with clear rollback procedures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: gateway token validation
function isTokenValid(token, tenantId) {
  const claims = parseToken(token);
  return claims.tenantId === tenantId && 
         claims.iss === 'https://sts.windows.net/' + tenantId &&
         claims.exp > Date.now() / 1000;
}

function validateRequest(req, tenantId) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token || !isTokenValid(token, tenantId)) {
    return { valid: false, reason: 'Invalid or missing token' };
  }
  return { valid: true, claims: parseToken(token) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply core Azure security controls (network, identities, policy) in a concrete, beginner-friendly scenario. It emphasizes least privilege, auditable changes, and rollback discipline.

## Key Concepts
- Private Endpoints and DENY public access for storage/sql
- User-Assigned Managed Identities for service-to-resource access
- Azure Policy to enforce private endpoints and deny public exposure
- Canary rollout and quick rollback plan with policy reversion

## Code Example
```json
// Policy (simplified) to deny public network access on storage and SQL
{
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "anyOf": [
        {"field": "type", "equals": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts"},
        {"field": "type", "equals": "Microsoft.Sql/servers"}
      ]
    },
    "then": {"effect": "deny"}
  }
}
```

